.TH NPROC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
nproc \- print the number of processors available
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nproc
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-ignore=\c
.IR N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nproc
prints the number of logical processors available to it, as a decimal
number on a line of its own on standard output.
The number is never less than 1.
Operands are ignored.
.PP
The count is the number of bits set in the process affinity mask, which
is the set of processors the process may run on.
An unrestricted mask holds every processor in the system, within the
limits given in NOTES.
A process inherits its mask from its parent, so it can be restricted, for
example by
.B start /affinity
in
.BR cmd.exe .
Only the affinity mask is consulted.
.SH OPTIONS
There are no short options.
Long options may be abbreviated to any unique prefix, so
.B \-\-al
means
.BR \-\-all ,
and
.B \-\-ig=1
means
.BR \-\-ignore=1 .
Options may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Count the processors in the system instead of those available to the
process.
This uses the system affinity mask in place of the process mask, so a
restricted process still counts every processor.
.B \-\-ignore
still applies.
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore= N
Subtract
.I N
from the count, but never go below 1.
.I N
is a nonnegative decimal integer no greater than 2147483647.
The argument may also be the next command line argument.
If the option is repeated, the last one applies.
.TP
.B \-\-help
When it is the first argument, print a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0, ignoring the other arguments.
In any other position it is an unknown option.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
Read by the C library's option parser.
When set, option parsing stops at the first operand, so options after it
are treated as operands and ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, a missing or invalid argument to
.BR \-\-ignore ,
or an argument given to
.BR \-\-all .
A message is written to standard error, followed by a usage message
unless the cause is an invalid
.B \-\-ignore
number.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run
.B make
with one job per processor:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make \-j"$(nproc)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Leave one processor free for other work:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make \-j"$(nproc \-\-ignore=1)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count the processors of a process restricted to the first two:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cmd /c start /affinity 3 /b /wait nproc
2
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR nproc :
there is no
.BR \-\-version ,
the
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
and
.B OMP_THREAD_LIMIT
variables are not read, and extra operands are ignored rather than
rejected.
.PP
The affinity mask is one machine word and covers one processor group, so
a 64-bit build counts at most 64 processors, and a 32-bit build at most
32.
On a system with more than 64 processors, Windows reports the mask of a
single processor group, or an empty mask when the process has threads in
several groups.
If the mask is empty or cannot be read, the count is 1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR make (1)
